/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a47bf94d/D10/0/code.py:19:# - Complexity calibration: restricting to a single cycle could become too narrow if the cycle order
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/19/code.py:16:#     - Keep the original objects: 3-cell L arrows and 1/3-pixel axis-aligned walls.
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/19/code.py:17:#     - Change 1 (D8): arrows do not fire independently. Sort them by squared distance from
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/19/code.py:18:#       the ray source (the arrow's right-angle vertex) to the grid center, nearest first.
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/19/code.py:23:#     - Example: arrow A near the center goes first, side-bounces off a teal wall at (7,8),
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/19/code.py:30:#     - Use exactly 2 arrows so the order cue is legible and the human can compare “near center”
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/19/code.py:32:#     - Force one static-wall bounce on the center-near arrow so wall erasure is always visible.
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/19/code.py:33:#     - Force one later trail collision on the farther arrow so the first-come-first-served rule
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/19/code.py:37:#     - Original `for start_col, pts in arrows:` assumed cross-arrow independence. This is invalid;
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/19/code.py:38:#       solver must sort arrows by center distance before tracing.
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/19/code.py:52:#     - Generator plans the near-center arrow output-first: one bounce, one wall, then an exit
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/19/code.py:53:#       segment. Its arrow cells, wall pixels, and full output trail are all disjoint.
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/19/code.py:54:#     - Generator then back-solves a second arrow whose straight path would next enter one chosen
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/19/code.py:55:#       cell from the first trail. The second arrow stops there, so its own painted cells remain
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/19/code.py:57:#     - The second arrow is constrained to be lexicographically earlier than the first source but
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/19/code.py:61:#     - Across examples, input shows a near-center arrow plus a farther arrow. Output shows a wall
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/19/code.py:62:#       gap on the near-center arrow's bounce and a truncated far-arrow tail near the first trail.
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/19/code.py:63:#     - Because the farther arrow is often visually earlier on the page, humans can infer that the
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/19/code.py:69:#     - Wall color differs from arrow colors so the post-bounce color change is structurally
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/19/code.py:72:#     - Solver assumes arrow source distances are orderable; generator rejects center-distance ties.
